=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive inspection of every layer — traffic control (tc qdiscs, ingress hooks, XDP), packet-filter rules (iptables, nftables), IP-layer counters (/proc/net/snmp InDiscards/OutDiscards), interface error counters, kernel policy routing, BGP state, and FRR forwarding tables — no packet loss mechanism of any kind is present in this lab at the time of investigation. All four devices (router1, router2, pc1, pc2) show zero dropped or discarded packets in every relevant counter. A sustained ping flood of 300 packets from pc1 to pc2 returned 0% loss; 100 normal pings in both directions also returned 0% loss; ICMP symmetry counters (492 sent = 492 received) confirm it. The BGP session between the two routers has been continuously stable for over nine minutes, with regular 60-second keepalives and no state transitions logged. The FRR RIB contains clean single-path entries with no ECMP, no null/blackhole routes, and no rejected prefixes. The only minor observation was 45 TCP retransmits in a 5-second, 47 MB iperf3 burst, representing 0.13% loss consistent with normal fq_codel active queue management behavior under a sudden burst — not a fault. The user's reported "one in three packets" lost cannot be reproduced under any test pattern. The most likely explanation is a transient condition during the BGP convergence window at lab startup (the first ~1–2 seconds before both routers install each other's prefixes), which would have caused a brief blackhole on the inter-AS path. No corrective action is required; the network is fully operational.
=== END ===